### When does ArrayList expand?

ArrayList expands only when the internal array is completely full and a new element needs to be added. The check happens inside the `add()` method: if `size == elementData.length`, call `grow()`. If there is still room in the array, the element is placed directly with no expansion.

This means that after expansion, there is always unused capacity. Adding the next few elements costs O(1) with no copying.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class ExpansionTriggerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>(5); // capacity 5

        // Adds 1-5: no expansion (room available)
        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
            accounts.add("ACC" + i);
            System.out.println("Added ACC" + i + " – size: " + accounts.size());
        }

        // Add 6: triggers expansion (capacity 5 -> 7)
        accounts.add("ACC6");
        System.out.println("Added ACC6 – size: " + accounts.size() + " (expansion occurred)");

        // Adds 7: no expansion (room in new array)
        accounts.add("ACC7");
        System.out.println("Added ACC7 – size: " + accounts.size());

        // Add 8: triggers expansion again (capacity 7 -> 10)
        accounts.add("ACC8");
        System.out.println("Added ACC8 – size: " + accounts.size() + " (expansion occurred)");
    }
}
```

```text
Added ACC1 – size: 1
Added ACC2 – size: 2
Added ACC3 – size: 3
Added ACC4 – size: 4
Added ACC5 – size: 5
Added ACC6 – size: 6 (expansion occurred)
Added ACC7 – size: 7
Added ACC8 – size: 8 (expansion occurred)
```

### What is the exact growth formula?

The formula from the OpenJDK source code is:

```java
int newCapacity = oldCapacity + (oldCapacity >> 1);
```

The `>> 1` operator is a bitwise right shift by 1, which is equivalent to integer division by 2. So the formula is `old + old/2 = 1.5 * old`.

Because integer division truncates, the actual growth is slightly less than 1.5x for odd numbers:

| Old capacity | old >> 1 | New capacity | Actual ratio |
|---|---|---|---|
| 10 | 5 | 15 | 1.50x |
| 15 | 7 | 22 | 1.47x |
| 22 | 11 | 33 | 1.50x |
| 33 | 16 | 49 | 1.48x |
| 49 | 24 | 73 | 1.49x |
| 73 | 36 | 109 | 1.49x |

The special case: when the ArrayList is created with the default constructor, the initial array is empty (size 0). On the first `add()`, the capacity jumps directly to 10 (the default capacity constant), not from 0 to 0 using the formula.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class GrowthFormulaDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Old Cap | >> 1 | New Cap | Ratio");
        int cap = 10;
        for (int i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
            int growth = cap >> 1;
            int newCap = cap + growth;
            System.out.printf("%7d | %4d | %7d | %.2fx%n", cap, growth, newCap, (double) newCap / cap);
            cap = newCap;
        }
    }
}
```

